WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:03.780
I’m Professor Dave, and I wanna teach you
how to speak Italian.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:11.720
Ciao, bello.

00:00:11.730 --> 00:00:13.530
So you’d like to learn Italian.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:15.070
Or at least you’re a little curious.

00:00:15.070 --> 00:00:19.100
It’s easy to see why you would be; there
are many reasons one would want to speak this

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:20.590
beautiful language.

00:00:20.590 --> 00:00:25.420
Perhaps you want to impress your date by reading
confidently from an Italian Restaurant menu.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:29.350
Maybe you want to see a Puccini opera and
know what the heck is going on.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:34.110
There are authors whose works you may want
to enjoy in the original language, like Dante

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.330
Alighieri.

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:39.940
You might want to watch films by the great
Italian directors, Fellini and Sorrentino,

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:43.399
without having to read poorly translated subtitles.

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:44.900
Maybe you need Italian for business.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:49.470
Italy’s major brands are recognized all
over the world, and many of them need employees

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:51.420
that speak the language.

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:53.920
Italy is also a leader in wine production.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.380
So a little Italian will definitely up your
sophistication.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.469
If none of these lofty pursuits fit the bill,
you’re either looking for a few phrases

00:01:01.469 --> 00:01:07.140
to use on your upcoming Italy trip, or you’re
taking Italian in school and you need a little help.

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.409
No matter what the reason, I’m here for
you.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:13.939
These tutorials will include fun vocabulary
that’s broken down by category, as well

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.859
as easy-to-understand explanations for all
the tricky grammar you will encounter.

00:01:18.859 --> 00:01:21.499
You can watch them all, or just the ones you
need.

00:01:21.499 --> 00:01:26.170
A hidden bonus that comes with learning Italian
is that since it’s a Romance language, meaning

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.990
it was derived from Latin, learning Italian
will make it much easier to learn any other

00:01:30.990 --> 00:01:37.210
Romance language, like Spanish, French, Portuguese,
or Romanian, all while challenging your brain

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.210
with something new.

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.759
And you’ll quickly be rewarded for your
efforts, because Italian is one of the easier

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:44.279
languages to learn.

00:01:44.279 --> 00:01:49.039
There are no disappearing consonants like
in French, and if you speak English, you don’t

00:01:49.039 --> 00:01:52.310
have to learn a new alphabet like with Chinese
or Arabic.

00:01:52.310 --> 00:01:58.060
All the letters and sounds are ones you already
know, so Italian phonetics are easy to pick up.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:01.180
There are more subtle benefits to learning
another language as well.

00:02:01.189 --> 00:02:06.299
Becoming familiar with how another culture
thinks and communicates will broaden your

00:02:06.299 --> 00:02:11.349
worldview, and expose you to emotions and
modes of thought that you never would have

00:02:11.349 --> 00:02:17.620
come across otherwise, and Italians certainly
know how to express themselves.

00:02:17.620 --> 00:02:23.750
Their language has a gorgeous musicality that
elevates even the most mundane phrase.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:27.079
Like this one: Oggi andiamo al mare.

00:02:27.079 --> 00:02:28.079
See?

00:02:28.079 --> 00:02:29.170
You’re learning already!

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.380
So grab a Campari and soda, sit back, and
let’s learn some Italian.

